jump_label: Sort the riscv64 jump table at build time

jump_label_init() sorts __jump_table at boot. With tracepoints there
are about 4000 entries, and heapsort with the relative swap function
takes about 4M instructions on riscv64.

Sort the table in scripts/sorttable for riscv64, whose jump labels are
relative (16-byte entries: code and target relative to themselves, the
key relative to itself with the flags in its low bits), by key and
then by code address as jump_label_cmp() does, and make
jump_label_sort_entries() return when the table is already sorted,
which costs one pass.

--- a/kernel/jump_label.c
+++ b/kernel/jump_label.c
@@ -79,7 +79,7 @@
 static void
 jump_label_sort_entries(struct jump_entry *start, struct jump_entry *stop)
 {
-	unsigned long size;
+	unsigned long size, i;
 	void *swapfn = NULL;
 
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HAVE_ARCH_JUMP_LABEL_RELATIVE))
@@ -87,6 +87,12 @@
 
 	size = (((unsigned long)stop - (unsigned long)start)
 					/ sizeof(struct jump_entry));
+	/* The build may have sorted the table already (scripts/sorttable) */
+	for (i = 1; i < size; i++)
+		if (jump_label_cmp(&start[i - 1], &start[i]) > 0)
+			break;
+	if (i >= size)
+		return;
 	sort(start, size, sizeof(struct jump_entry), jump_label_cmp, swapfn);
 }
 
